WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.900
But yeah, vehicles are very long.

2
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.900
They have long complex development cycles.

3
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.900
And now that you're adding things like the legislation

4
00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.900
and compliance activities to that, it gets a little bit longer,

5
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.900
and there's a lot more that has to go into there.

6
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:14.900
And also something big I want to mention is this is still a very niche industry.

7
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.900
It's only really been around for about 10 or so years.

8
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.900
And I'll talk a little bit about the timeline in just a second, right?

9
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:26.700
So a long time ago, when they began to incorporate electronics

10
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.700
into vehicles and the vehicle networking and older technologies

11
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.700
like the CAN bus in the 1980s, this is really when people started

12
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.700
experimenting with their cars as computers

13
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.700
and learning about the networking technologies

14
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.700
and the software that's used in vehicles.

15
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.700
And this is my own personal reflection of the way this went.

16
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.700
This is not an official published timeline or anything, right?

17
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.700
But I like to call this the prehistoric era.

18
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.700
As people were doing cybersecurity research on vehicles,

19
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.700
finding vulnerabilities, quirks in the way these networks operated.

20
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:56.700
Oh, hey, CAN bus has no source address, has no destination,

21
00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:59.700
no encryption, no authentication.

22
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:00.700
This is kind of funny.

23
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.700
It's like, well, yeah, but it's a wired network.

24
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.700
You can't actually hack it from outside the car.

25
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.700
So the only way you could hack a CAN bus

26
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.700
is by getting physical access to the vehicle.

27
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.700
And at that point, you're already inside the car.

28
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.700
So who cares, right?

29
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.700
And this is a very real stance the industry had taken.

30
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:23.700
Until July 21, 2015, is what I like to call the ground zero

31
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.700
for the automotive cybersecurity industry.

32
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.700
This is an event called the Jeep Hack.

33
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.700
If you Google Jeep Hack,

34
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.700
and many of you may have already heard of this before,

35
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.200
you can find some news articles and YouTube videos

36
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.200
published by Wired, Wired Magazine,

37
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.200
in which they actually interviewed two security researchers

38
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.200
who spent two years reverse engineering

39
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.200
and studying the computer systems

40
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.200
and the cloud system of the Uconnect

41
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.200
that was using the Jeep vehicle line

42
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.200
for about two years.

43
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.200
And they found that there were so many vulnerabilities

44
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.200
in the design and implementation of the technologies

45
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.200
used in these Jeep Wranglers,

46
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.200
or excuse me, the Jeep Cherokees,

47
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.200
that they could actually remotely control

48
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.200
and kill the engine of a Jeep Cherokee

49
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.200
from anywhere in the United States

50
00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.200
without ever touching the vehicle.

51
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.200
All they needed to know was the vehicle's VIN,

52
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.200
the vehicle identification number,

53
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.200
and they could commit this kind of like DOS attack

54
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.200
without ever laying a finger on the vehicle.

55
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.200
This kind of shook up the whole industry, right?

56
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.200
I think that at the time, FCA, Fiat Chrysler,

57
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.200
tried to basically pay off the researchers,

58
00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.200
like we'll give you each a million dollars,

59
00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.200
do not publish this research kind of thing.

60
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.200
And they kind of went louder than anybody.

61
00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.200
They said, no.

62
00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.200
So we have now the modern automotive cybersecurity industry

63
00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.200
thanks to their bravery.

64
00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.200
And I think what they did was very important.

65
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.200
They went ahead with the responsible disclosure.

66
00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.200
They informed the manufacturers

67
00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:52.200
that they had found these vulnerabilities.

68
00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:53.200
They had them fixed before they published this.

69
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.200
But they weren't doing this work under NDA.

70
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.200
They were doing this as independent research.

71
00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.200
And so they did not have the obligation

72
00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:02.200
that they had to keep their findings secret,

73
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.200
which is kind of a quirk about working in this industry,

74
00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.200
depending on how you want to do testing on these.

75
00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.200
But after this, you had kind of like a panic or gold rush

76
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.200
where everyone's trying to figure out how to secure vehicles.

77
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.200
Companies are making solutions for in-vehicle network,

78
00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.200
intrusion detection systems,

79
00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.200
intrusion prevention systems, et cetera.

80
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.200
A lot of those companies still exist,

81
00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.200
and a lot of them have products that work to some degree.

82
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.200
But no one really knew what they were doing.

83
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.200
And the automotive industry wasn't very eager

84
00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.200
to accept cybersecurity

85
00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.200
because they didn't really understand its value

86
00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:38.200
and because this is very relevant

87
00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.200
to the way the automotive industry works.

88
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.200
There's no compulsion

89
00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.200
or kind of mandatory motivation

90
00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.200
for them to invest in cybersecurity.

91
00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.200
To give some context, at the time,

92
00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.200
during the years of 2017, 2018,

93
00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.200
I was working for a small company

94
00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:58.200
that was started in Japan,

95
00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.200
and we were doing cybersecurity for vehicles.

96
00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:02.200
We had invented some encryption technology

97
00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.200
for vehicle network systems.

98
00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.200
That was kind of novel and very lightweight

99
00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:07.200
and very applicable.

100
00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.200
And basically, we had a whole meeting

101
00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:12.200
with one of the higher-ups in Toyota Motor Company.

102
00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.200
And we showed them the demos

103
00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.200
and the examples of how vehicles can be hacked

104
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:18.200
and how this is a serious safety risk.

105
00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.200
And, you know, the guy basically told us,

106
00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:21.200
like, this is incredible, you know,

107
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.200
very amazing technologically, very cool,

108
00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:26.200
but I'm not going to spend a penny on this

109
00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.200
until the government says that I have to.

110
00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.200
And because that's kind of the way

111
00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:31.200
the automotive industry works.

112
00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.200
And I think many other industries do this too,

113
00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.200
but especially because for the automotive industry,

114
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:38.200
you know, they have very strict safety guidelines.

115
00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:40.200
And when it comes to automotive safety testing and stuff,

116
00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.200
it's very much a,

117
00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.200
you do it once, and it's kind of done.

118
00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.200
You can do it properly, and it's safe.

119
00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.200
They've got functional safety standards

120
00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:50.200
that, like, once you pass those standards,

121
00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:53.200
they're very good, they're very well established.

122
00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.200
There's not a lot of risk of, like,

123
00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.200
safety problems popping up later.

124
00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:58.200
As opposed to cybersecurity,

125
00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.200
an industry where new vulnerabilities

126
00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:01.200
are discovered every single day,

127
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.200
new attack patterns are discovered every single day,

128
00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.200
we find, oh, hey, supply chain attack.

129
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.200
This, you know, piece of software

130
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.200
has been maintained by this one dude by himself.

131
00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.200
Well, someone started adding code

132
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.200
that was malicious and obfuscated,

133
00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.200
and every version of the operating system

134
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.200
from this year on forward has this vulnerability.

135
00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:19.200
These kind of things, you know, pop up in cybersecurity,

136
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.200
and it's very much a dynamic activity.

137
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:23.200
It's not just a do it once and it's done.

138
00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.200
It's a kind of a, you know,

139
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.200
very different type of activity

140
00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.200
from what the automotive industry was used to.

141
00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.200
And because there was no legislation

142
00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.200
or legal motivation for them to kind of invest

143
00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.200
in this industry,

144
00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:37.200
it was very much,

145
00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.200
do we really need it?

146
00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:40.000
I don't really think we do kind of thing.
